{
  "correction_rules": [
    "Persistent positive bias: model forecasts are consistently above realized prices across most horizons, especially after day ~8–10. Apply a downward level shift (≈1–3 EUR in the first week, widening to ≈5–10 EUR by weeks 3–4 in downtrends).",
    "Trend underreaction / lag: when history is in a clear selloff, the model projects stabilization or rebound too early. Add stronger negative drift proportional to the last 10–20 day slope; do not assume mean reversion until the slope flattens.",
    "Horizon-dependent overshoot: forecast paths often rise into the mid-horizon (days ~10–20) while the true series continues falling. Damp mid-horizon rebounds; cap cumulative upside vs day-1 forecast unless recent returns are positive.",
    "Mean-reversion miscalibration: model mean reverts upward too aggressively after declines. Reduce mean-reversion strength when the last 5–15 days are negative and volatility is elevated; allow continuation regimes.",
    "Spike/rebound overestimation: after short-term upticks in history, the model extrapolates a larger rebound than realized. Attenuate response to 1–3 day positive moves (e.g., scale recent positive returns by ~0.5) unless confirmed by a higher high over ~5 days."
  ]
}
